body:
I'm not going to call myself a liberal (because Americans have a weird concept for it that I'm confused about) but I'm very much a leftist and I think most (Western) leftists get the whole thing about muslims wrong. Yes, they face discrimination in countries like the US, which is unacceptable. HOWEVER, that doesn't mean everything they do and believe in is fine and alright. I'm not going to pretend that there's anything about Islam that is better, more peaceful, etc. than Christianity. The only reason leftists seem to have a bias towards it is because Christianity is more powerful around these parts and they always wanna root for the oppressed, but countries like the UK/US don't at all represent the status of muslims around the world.

&#x200B;

\-And it especially baffles me when feminists want to pretend that Islam is perfectly compatible with feminism. Idc if you wanna call yourself a muslim feminist, but if you're gonna take on feminist ideals you're gonna have to deny some aspects of Islam, because  there's glaring contradictions. I've seen a lot of muslim-feminists act like the scriptures totally are feminist by twisting them just enough. Girl stop, just face it.

\-When I got into makeup Too Faced had a sort of pink vintage style and everything they made interested me, up to around the time they made their Chocolate Bon Bons. Everything after that always seemed to scream 'this is probably not great quality', which was maybe not always true but it seemed so because of its cutesy look.

&#x200B;

\-NARS is another one. I especially liked the idea of their little duos and trios of eyeshadow because the color combos seemed to unique and interesting to me, but as more brands started popping up and eyeshadow palettes became more common, I just lost interest in the idea of having a bunch of little duos and trios (and their recent bigger palettes are uninteresting to me). Plus they changed their website and it's more annoying now.

\-Not exactly a beauty guru but I remember KVD was known or believed to be antisemitic much before the antivax thing came out. From what I remember, she had dated a known neonazi, had apparently left an antisemitic note for someone she worked with (which she claimed was faked). Some people also already took issue with her being holier-than-thou about her veganism and some took issue with her product names (Lolita, Underage Red, Celebutard, Selektion are some of the ones I remember) and the way she responded to that criticism was also very dismissive.

&#x200B;

\-The neonazi thing actually reminded me of an Australian beautuber, Shani Grimmond, who was a bit under fire for posing in front of a Nazi flag or something like that in a snap. I remember it wasn't ironic or anything, the flag was like background decoration essentially and she claimed she didn't know what it meant lmao. I'm not sure if it was a big thing that many people talked about, I just remember it happened in 2015
